vich–Sher branch is at v2 with minor revisions and 42 pages. Concurrent proofs improve confidence but do not replace specialist assessment.\\n\\nSources and record details\\n\\nStable ID: `math-polya-neumann-euclidean-balls-2026` · Permanent record URL\\n\\n  * Primary Yutian Li, Pólya’s Conjecture for the Neumann Eigenvalues on Euclidean Balls\\n  * Independent preprint Filonov, Levitin, Polterovich and Sher, Pólya’s conjecture for higher-dimensional Neumann balls\\n  * Published context Filonov, Levitin, Polterovich and Sher, published Dirichlet-ball and Neumann-disk proof\\n\\nAI involvement: Li discloses no AI role in the inspected manuscript. Filonov, Levitin, Polterovich and Sher say ChatGPT and Claude contributed to several technical lemmas and the design and implementation of their computational algorithm; they independently checked the work and accept responsibility.\\n\\nChange history\\n\\n  * 2026-07-29 — Added as a provisional claimed proof after full-text review; the ball-only scope, first-version status and unreviewed finite calculations are explicit.\\n  * 2026-08-08 — Reviewed Li’s substantially revised v3 and added a concurrent independent preprint by Filonov, Levitin, Polterovich and Sher. The active record now discloses the second group’s substantive AI contribution; status remains provisional.\\n  * 2026-10-02 — Reviewed the independent branch’s minor v2 revision; no theorem or status change found.\\n\\nMathematics · Operator theory, functional analysis and numerical ranges Checked 2026-11-04\\n\\n### Crouzeix proof claims gain a named specialist assessment\\n\\nClaimed proof with named specialist assessment Evidence tier 1 Peer reviewed: No AI role: Yes\\n\\nTwo preprints give independent arguments for the sharp constant two in Crouzeix’s inequality: for a matrix or bounded operator A and an appropriate function f, the operator norm of f(A) is at most twice the supremum of |f| on A’s numerical range. Townsend and Greenbaum report that they and Michel Crouzeix thoroughly checked Jin’s proof and believe it correct; their assessment appeared in SIAM News on 2 November, with an earlier version dated 15 August.\\n\\nProblem or result\\n    Crouzeix’s conjectured optimal constant two for functions of matrices and operators\\n\\nAuthors\\n    Emiel Lorist and Felix Schwenninger; independently, Shanmu Jin\\n\\nInstitutions\\n    Delft University of Technology and University of Twente; Jin: Peking Union Medical College Hospital, as reported by Townsend and Greenbaum.\\n\\nResult date\\n    Lorist–Schwenninger preprint submitted August 4; Jin manuscript revised through August 7, 2026\\n\\nWhy it matters: The conjecture has been a central sharp-bound problem connecting non-normal matrices, spectral sets and numerical analysis. The factor two is known to be optimal, so a correct proof would close the remaining gap from the published 1 + square-root-of-two bound.\\n\\nLimits: The papers remain preprints, and Jin’s repository says formal peer review is pending. The named specialist assessment is outside corroboration, not journal publication or a Kingy proof or formalization audit. The assessment predates its 2 November SIAM publication.\\n\\nSources and record details\\n\\nStable ID: `math-crouzeix-conjecture-proof-2026` · Permanent record URL\\n\\n  * Primary Lorist and Schwenninger, A solution to Crouzeix’s conjecture\\n  * Independent preprint Shanmu Jin, The Crouzeix Conjecture\\n  * Published context Crouzeix and Palencia, the earlier 1 + square-root-of-two bound\\n  * Specialist assessment Townsend and Greenbaum, SIAM News specialist assessment\\n  * Specialist assessment Townsend and Greenbaum, earlier assessment dated 15 August\\n  * Primary Jin, author repository and review status\\n\\nAI involvement: Lorist and Schwenninger disclose GPT-5.6 Sol Pro proof-strategy assistance. Townsend and Greenbaum report that Jin’s decisive theorem emerged during an approximately 16-hour autonomous ChatGPT-5.6 Sol run; Jin’s