erry, Havet, Linhares Sales, Reed and Thomassé, and give a substantially simplified proof of an analogous result for eulerian digraphs, again proved by GPT-6 Astra, and announced by Mubayi and Verstraete. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFull Text Articles available from EurekaMag Chapter 98669 (https://apps.eurekamag.com/research/098/668/)\\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; 2025: Neural Codes and Neural Ring Endomorphisms.\\nRocky Mountain Journal of Mathematics 55(4): 989-995】Gupta, N.; Suhith, K.N. 2025: Neural Codes and Neural Ring Endomorphisms. Rocky Mountain Journal of Mathematics 55(4): 997-1022Ya, Z.; Yucai, X.; Huicong, C.; Huang, S.; Liangliang, M.; Gang, J.; Weilong, W.; Huang, X. 2023: Effects of different diets on growth, reproduction, and population dynamics of Daphnia carinata; 不同饵料对隆线溞生长繁殖及种群动态的影响. Journal of Fishery Sciences of China 30(5): 643-654Xu, Z.; Sui, D.; Xie, Y.; Wang, J. 2021: Comparison of photosynthetic characteristics of two new Triadica sebifera varieties. Journal of Nanjing Forestry University (Natural Sciences Edition) 45(1): 93-100Sui, X.; Zhao, Q.; Zhou, X.; Chen, J.; Chen, J.; Peng, Q.; Zhang, Z. 2024: Response of plant diversity under Platycladus orientalis plantation to canopy density in Xuzhou; 徐州市侧柏人工林林下植物多样性对郁闭度的响应. Journal of Nanjing Forestry University (Natural Sciences Edition) 48(4): 219-226Jingwen, X.; Fangfang, W.; Xin, Z.; Ruifeng, P.; Xiaoye, W.; Xiaoru, M. 2024: Effect of ganoderma spore on mitochondrial autophagy and apoptosis in testicular tissue of diabetic rats. Chinese Journal of Tissue Engineering Research 28(4): 562-568【659†Ekejiuba, A.I.B.; Nwachukwu, A.N.; Duru, U.I.; Ekwueme, S.T.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-09-02&page=135&range=31)\\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;   * Generalised Cone Conjecture, II: Generic Sakai surfaces\\n  * Spin preservation in screw-symmetric molecules\\n\\nJonas Bloch, Fedor Baranov, Maxim Breitkreiz\\n\\nAug 28 2026 cond-mat.mes-hall arXiv:2608.27002v1\\n\\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\\n\\n0\\n\\n@misc{2608.27002, author = {Jonas Bloch and Fedor Baranov and Maxim Breitkreiz}, title = {{S}pin preservation in screw-symmetric molecules}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2608.27002}, note = {arXiv:2608.27002v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\\n\\nPDF\\n\\nIn electronic transport through long molecules, spin is expected to be preserved only when the dwell time is much shorter than the characteristic spin-mixing timescale $\\\\hbar/\\\\Delta$, where $\\\\Delta$ is the magnitude of a spin-dependent potential, such as spin-orbit coupling. We show that, in molecules featuring discrete screw symmetry, spin preservation can be enhanced far beyond this timescale owing to strong spin separation in quasi-momentum. This spin fidelity in long molecules is consistent with chirality-induced spin selectivity (CISS), suggesting spin-dependent transport in long, chiral molecules with amplified spin-splitting mechanisms. We provide analytical derivation of the enhanced spin preservation and test it on tight-binding models, which confirm that the effect gradually weakens when the screw symmetry is broken or changes from discrete to continuous. Furthermore, we perform transport simulations to show that a strong magnetoresistance trace of symmetry-protected spin fidelity emerges in a spin-valve setup with two magnetic leads, which we propose as an experimentally accessible signature.\\n\\n  * Generalised Cone Conjecture, II: Generic Sakai surfaces\\n\\nVladimir Lazić, Isabel Stenger, Zhixin Xie\\n\\nAug 28 2026 math.AG arXiv:2608.27001v1\\n\\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\\n\\n0\\n\\n@misc{2608.27001, author = {Vladimir Lazić and Isabel Stenger and Zhixin Xie}, title = {{G}eneralised {C}one {C}onjecture, {II}: {G}eneric {S}akai surfaces}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2608.27001}, note = {arXiv:2608.27001v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-09-12&page=162&range=31)\\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Forsythe's conjecture, published in 1968, asserts that for each restart length $s$, every exact-arithmetic restarted conjugate-gradient iteration on a real symmetric positive definite problem either terminates or has normalised residuals that converge separately along the even and odd restart subsequences. ... Together with Akaike's theorem for $s=1$, this shows that the conjectured universal conclusion is true precisely for $s\\\\in\\\\{1,2,3\\\\}$ and false for every $s\\\\ge4$.\\nWe further define a $g$-twisted bicategorical trace that recovers the $g$-twisted Hattori--Stallings trace. For $C_n$-Green functors, the orbitwise $g$-twisted Hattori--Stallings traces assemble into a morphism of $C_n$-Mackey functors that, at the orbit $C_n/C_n$, recovers the degree-zero twisted Dennis trace.\\n\\n  * A Complete Resolution of Forsythe's Conjecture for Restarted Conjugate Gradients\\n\\nMatthew J. Colbrook, George Stepaniants, Alex Townsend\\n\\nSep 07 2026 math.NA cs.NA math.DS math.OC arXiv:2609.04659v2\\n\\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\\n\\n0\\n\\n@misc{2609.04659, author = {Matthew J.~Colbrook and George Stepaniants and Alex Townsend}, title = {{A} {C}omplete {R}esolution of {F}orsythe's {C}onjecture for {R}estarted {C}onjugate {G}radients}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2609.04659}, doi = {10.5281/zenodo.22557261}, note = {arXiv:2609.04659v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\\n\\nPDF\\n\\nForsythe's conjecture, published in 1968, asserts that for each restart length $s$, every exact-arithmetic restarted conjugate-gradient iteration on a real symmetric positive definite problem either terminates or has normalised residuals that converge separately along the even and odd restart subsequences. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nau:N_S in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AN_S+in%3Amath)\\nciteturn0search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Jul 31 2026 cs.DM cs.CC math.CO arXiv:2607.27802v1 ... We exploit the truncated singular value decomposition and the recently proposed circulant decomposition for an efficient first-order approximation of the multiplication of large dense matrices.\\nFinally, we illustrate our methods using individual-level data from the Panel Study of Income Dynamics (PSID) survey from the US as well as Indian household level income data for different states sourced from the Consumer Pyramids Household Survey (CPHS).\\n\\n  * Efficient approximations of matrix multiplication using truncated decompositions\\n\\nSuvendu Kar, Hariprasad M., Sai Gowri J. N., Murugesan Venkatapathi\\n\\nApr 29 2025 math.NA cs.NA arXiv:2504.19308v4\\n\\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\\n\\n0\\n\\n@misc{2504.19308, author = {Suvendu Kar and Hariprasad M.~and Sai Gowri J.~N.~and Murugesan Venkatapathi}, title = {{E}fficient approximations of matrix multiplication using truncated decompositions}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2504.19308}, note = {arXiv:2504.19308v4} }[Button: Copy Citation]\\n\\nPDF\\n\\nWe exploit the truncated singular value decomposition and the recently proposed circulant decomposition for an efficient first-order approximation of the multiplication of large dense matrices. A decomposition of each matrix into a sum of a sparse matrix with relatively few dominant entries and a dense residue can also use the above approach, and we present methods for multiplication using a Fourier decomposition and a cycle decomposition-based sparsifications. The proposed methods scale as $\\\\mathcal{O}(n^2 \\\\log n)$ in arithmetic operations for $n \\\\times n$ matrices for usable tolerances in relative error $\\\\sim$ 1\\\\%. We also present demonstrations of large gains in the efficiency and speed of end-to-end operations of Large Language Models (LLMs) as a motivation. Note that different decompositions for the two matrices $A$ and $B$ in the product $AB$ are also possible in this approach, using efficient a priori evaluations for suitability, to improve further on the error tolerances demonstrated here.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nTrace — Mon 2 Nov — AI Edge Briefing (https://aiedgebriefing.com/2026-11-02/trace/)\\nciteturn0search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 days ago; Crawled: yesterday; The only timestamps are on reader comments, which run from 10:59 am to 9:14 pm on 1 November, and the first comment dated 2 November.\\\\n\\\\n**Summary of claims**\\\\n\\\\n- Calegari argues that AI can now make groundbreaking mathematical contributions, and that mathematicians should respond constructively rather than with despair.\\\\n- He credits the OpenAI release of over 700 research papers as a turning point, though he criticizes the papers' writing, attribution, and correctness.\\\\n- He compares the situation to the classification of finite simple groups, arguing that solving a famous problem doesn't end a field.\\\\n- He argues that mathematicians should pursue broader programs and structural understanding, and that AI opens space for more ambitious research plans.\\\\n- He says work should be evaluated as if AI-assisted, and that a paper should explain why its mathematics matters.\\\\n- His six proposals are: evaluate work as if AI-assisted; hire through personal interaction and interviews; demand more from journals; broaden graduate training; give better seminars; and take ownership of AI-produced results.\\\\n- He argues that many arXiv papers should not be published in journals at all, and that Lean verification and tools like hexagonmath can help.\\\\n\\\\n**Numbers, named systems, and proposals**\\\\n\\\\n- **OpenAI:** released over 700 research papers on 6 October 2026.\\\\n- **Hexagonmath** (hexagonmath.org): cited as a place to record autonomously AI-produced, ideally Lean-checked, proofs.\\\\n- **Lean:** mentioned as a verification standard for papers.\\\\n- **Leiden declaration:** said to be \\\\\\\"honored more in name than in spirit.\\\\\\\"\\\\n- **Essential Number Theory:** cited as a journal worth keeping for its emphasis on utility and clarity.\\\\n- **Nelson Cole:** factored 2^67 − 1 = 193707721 · 761838257287 over \\\\\\\"three years of Sundays\\\\\\\" in 1903.\\\\n- **Christian Szegedy:** quoted on how mathematical skills may shift toward choosing terrain and explaining findings.\\\\n- **Hugo Duminil-Copin:** quoted lamenting that his open problems were all claimed as solved.\\\\n- **3Blue1Brown:** cited as having over six million subscribers.\\\\n- **Gorenstein:** announced the finite simple groups program in 1972 and the classification in 1983.\\\\n\\\\n**Key quotes (each under 125 characters)**\\\\n\\\\n- \\\\\\\"The release by OpenAI on October 6 of over 700 research papers was a watershed in the history of mathematics.\\\\\\\"\\\\n- \\\\\\\"Choosing worthwhile problems may end up mattering more than producing proofs.\\\\\\\"\\\\n- \\\\\\\"If you, as a senior mathematician, are stressed about being scooped by AI, then you are not being ambitious enough.\\\\\\\"\\\\n- \\\\\\\"The difference in reaction between the optimists and the pessimists is that, for the pessimists, the game is over. ... Bellwood-QoE partial checks: GPT-6.1 Sol averaged 5.6 of 11, Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra averaged 5.4, GLM 5.3 and Grok 4.7 each averaged under 2, and the best single run passed 8 of 11.\\\\n- Legal: binary scores were 0% across the board.\\n11:10:20\\n\\nWebFetch https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/11/01/mathematics-has-changed-lets-act-like-it/ 9263 ms\\n\\ninput\\n    \\n    {\\n      \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/11/01/mathematics-has-changed-lets-act-like-it/\\\",\\n      \\\"prompt\\\": \\\"Give the exact publication date and time shown, 